 opening up the party to the  diversity and overused word now but to  the diversity that we saw at the  democratic convention in 1992. just five  minutes left there's a lot more in here  in this book but let me jump to the end  and we've got some  you've got some pictures here this is a  picture of the funeral what was her life  like  in the last several years  again what year did she die 1977.  her life was i think one of very great  frustration in her last three or four  years she wasn't well  she had hypertension she had a nervous  breakdown at one point i'm sure from  overwork  uh she but she kept on going them it was  extraordinary  um  she  had very poor health care  she developed breast cancer  and had surgery for that in 1976  but she stayed active she  helped bring low-cost housing to her  hometown  she sued the local school system  i helped save the job of the black  principal in the high school there  she  her dream was a farm co-op that would  help people  have food produce their own food so they  could have some independence from the  man and she put it from the plantation  owner she stayed active she  i think five months before she died she  went to jackson for a demonstration  about cuts in medicare  so she  wasn't well i think she  found  mississippi was resisting  the full enforcement of the voting  rights act this frustrated her  enormously so she  uh felt  somewhat abandoned that things were  moving on without her  so she um  i guess  i get sort of sad when i think that she  didn't live to see  the changes that have happened  today she would have loved  i have to tell this story i was in  ruralville mississippi very recently  and at speaking at the public library  that 30 years ago she couldn't use  and  at the invitation of the librarian from  sunflower county  and  a  the new state senator black man named  willie simmons stood up he had defeated  the man who defeated fannie lou hamer in  1971 when she ran for state senate  willie simmons was the first black  senator from sunflower county he stood  up and said  i am living fannie lou hamer's dream  well a pretty powerful moment  here is the  tombstone and you can't see it clearly  on the screen but it says there i'm sick  and tired of being sick and tired uh you  read that a lot throughout your book  where did that come from she  said it a lot she said it to an  interviewer in about 1964.  she would say it  in speeches  all over and she meant it literally i am  sick and tired of being sick and tired  and and and all of the people were tired  of this of course i was  extremely excited last summer during the  1992  democratic convention when bill clinton  quoted fannie lou hamer saying i think  he said something like if like fannie  lou hamer you're sick and tired of being  sick and tired then whatever it was he  was proposing  went on i didn't hear what he was  proposing because i got so excited about  what he said that he mentioned fannie  lou hamer so  you know she is known by  at least certainly a lot of the  southerners who came of age  during that movement what do you hope  this book will do  i hope it will show young people that  one person can make a difference  next book for you  i'm about to sign a contract with dutton  for  a primer on women's history  other books you want to write  yes i'm not quite sure yet  i don't know when i'll get as good a  subject as this again  but i'm sure going to be looking would  you rather write biography or policy  oriented stuff i think right now i'm a  little um  i've been so immersed in her life i'd  like to do some policy stuff until i  find uh  another  this little light of mine  here's what the cover looks like and our  guest has been kay mills  who's now a freelance writer used to be  with the los angeles times editorial  board the name of the book is this  little light of mine  the life of fannie lou hamer thank you  very much thank you brian  [Music]  next week on booknotes our guest will be  alex dragneesh who served as the public  affairs officer at the u.s embassy in  the former republic of yugoslavia he'll  discuss his new book serbs and croats  the struggle in yugoslavia  book notes can be seen each sunday  evening on c-span at 8 p.m eastern time  and again at 11 pm eastern time the  program can also be seen each monday  morning beginning at 6 55 a.m eastern  time  coming up in a moment prime minister's  question time from the british house of  commons  [Music]
 

